Before you implement, please fix these points:

1. NO-BUDGET RULE: Use one single rule. An expense is "budgeted" if it was
   counted into projects.budget_consumed (i.e. it went through
   BudgetGuardService). An expense is "associated" if it has this project_id
   but was NOT counted there. Show me exactly where and when budget_consumed
   is incremented for each source, and base the filter on that. Do not use
   budget_status IS NULL as the rule.

2. DOUBLE COUNTING: Check whether MaterialUsage (warehouse dispatch) is the
   same material already counted through ProcurementPayment, PettyCash, or
   CreditStore. If so, do not add both. Tell me your decision and why.
   Also check whether a paid ExpenseRequest creates an Expense row, and make
   sure each real expense appears only once.

3. SOURCES: The status table lists 6 sources but Step 1 lists 5. Confirm the
   final list. Include Expense only if it is not a duplicate of ExpenseRequest.

4. STATUS TIMING: Use the same status/moment that the budget uses to consume
   budget, so nothing falls between "budgeted" and "associated". For
   CreditStoreLedger, state whether you use the paid amount or the full amount.

5. PERFORMANCE: Do filtering, sorting, and pagination in SQL (UNION or
   per-source queries with limits), not by loading everything into a PHP
   collection.

6. FILES: Name the exact Blade files you will edit (the plan lists
   show.blade.php twice).

7. TESTS: Add one more test: an expense counted in budget_consumed must NOT
   appear in Associated Costs (no double counting).

Show me the final "no budget" rule and the source list, then proceed.
Keep Labor, IPCs, and Fuel out for now.